 CellLabel->"In[1]:=",
 CellID->904125756,ExpressionUUID->"8ad9d452-2e30-4456-a46b-e0afaf4735f7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Algebra with this product alone makes so called ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       SubscriptBox[
        StyleBox["K", "TI"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", 
          StyleBox["q", "TI"]}]], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "K_{p,q}", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "13cd7051-3c8b-46d8-9a34-f3c9fdf618c7"],
 " algebras. These algebras also are called algebras of multiple imaginary \
units, where each basis vector represents different imaginary unit. In \
Clifford algebra, however, we can introduce a new commutative product and \
define a field with multiple commutative units as (same or distinct) Clifford \
algebra. The construction, therefore, will result into Clifford algebra over \
field with multiple imaginary units.  ",
 StyleBox["Only algebra of multiple imaginary units is implemented at the \
moment, but not multiple imaginary unit field over Clifford algebra.",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]
}], "Text",
 Editable->True,
 TaggingRules->{},
